1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The International Space Station,

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:08,800
a permanent platform in space, a stepping stone

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:12,740
to the solar system, a scientific research center.

4
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:16,240
Now, Space Stations on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,540
The Russian rocket blasts into space

6
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:35,980
carrying a special Russian-made module.

7
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:41,180
Two weeks later, the shuttle Endeavour lifts off,

8
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:43,840
carrying an American module.

9
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:47,960
December 6, 1998, the shuttle catches up to the Russian

10
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:49,060
ship.

11
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:50,460
The shuttle is a ship.

12
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:51,960
The ship is a ship.

13
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:01,360
the shuttle catches up to the Russian section in space

14
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:04,400
and connects the two modules.

15
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:10,500
The International Space Station becomes a reality.

16
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:12,440
In the years to come, the space station

17
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:17,640
will increase in size to more than a million pounds.

18
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:20,780
This is the largest peacetime collaborative effort

19
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:21,920
in history.

20
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:26,560
16 countries, including Japan, Russia, Brazil, Canada,

21
00:01:26,561 --> 00:01:30,640
And the United States are united in a single goal,

22
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:34,060
to build the first international space station

23
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:39,640
and secure humankind's permanent presence in space.

24
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:44,180
Each country is contributing technology and resources.

25
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:46,180
But a majority of the hardware is coming

26
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:50,680
from the United States and Russia, historically unlikely

27
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:51,480
partners.

28
00:01:51,481 --> 00:01:55,820
The birth of the International Space Station

29
00:01:55,920 --> 00:02:00,160
began with an announcement in 1984 by President Ronald Reagan.

30
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:03,300
Tonight, I am directing NASA to develop a permanently manned

31
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:06,320
space station and to do it within a decade.

32
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:11,640
Setbacks, budget cuts, and redesigns

33
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:16,940
delayed Reagan's dream of space station completion in 10 years.

34
00:02:16,941 --> 00:02:20,540
But today's redesigned and smaller ISS still

35
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:23,740
has the same purpose, research.

36
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:29,680
Six international research laboratories

37
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:33,180
will make up the bulk of the station.

38
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:36,400
Scientists from around the world will perform experiments

39
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:40,300
in space to benefit people on Earth.

40
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:44,700
The ISS promises advances in such fields as medicine

41
00:02:44,701 --> 00:02:46,700
and metallurgy.

42
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:49,700
To support this research, the ISS will also

43
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:54,340
be comprised of service, control, and habitation modules

44
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:57,440
containing life support, navigation, communication,

45
00:02:57,540 --> 00:02:58,640
and propulsion systems.

46
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:07,220
Solar arrays on giant trusses will stretch 360 by 290 feet

47
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:10,520
to collect energy from the sun.

48
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:13,100
The construction of the International Space Station

49
00:03:13,101 --> 00:03:16,800
will require over 46 separate launches of equipment

50
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:19,700
and supplies.

51
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:23,740
Orbiting the Earth at 17,500 miles per hour,

52
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:27,140
the station will be assembled in the harsh environment

53
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:29,140
of outer space.

54
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:32,620
The intent is to provide an absolutely world-class research

55
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:36,520
facility that we can operate at least for 15 years.

56
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:38,900
The space station has lofty goals

57
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,560
and an even loftier price tag.

58
00:03:41,561 --> 00:03:44,900
The $40 billion projected cost has led critics

59
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,560
to question the merit of the station.

60
00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:52,900
But money hasn't been the only trouble for the ISS program.

61
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,080
Two near-fatal incidents aboard the Russian Mir space station

62
00:03:57,180 --> 00:04:00,080
during the preliminary stages of the cooperation

63
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:03,080
threatened the program.

64
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:04,320
He came in too fast.

65
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:07,060
By the time he started breaking, it was too late,

66
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:09,520
and he began shouting again, get to the Soyuz,

67
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:11,160
get to the Soyuz, we have to evacuate.

68
00:04:11,161 --> 00:04:12,420
And then it hit.

69
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:14,660
The story of the International Space Station

70
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:19,340
is filled with dramatic highs and lows, budget cuts,

71
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:21,900
a new president, and tragedy.

72
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,800
All could have left the space station

73
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:27,780
on the scrap heap of history instead of above our heads.

74
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:34,020
It's a story that begins centuries ago.

75
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:39,680
Since the dawn of humankind, the question

76
00:04:39,681 --> 00:04:42,320
the question of what lay beyond the clouds

77
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:44,820
has fueled imaginations.

78
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:49,120
Gods, monsters, alien beings.

79
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:52,620
People endeavored not only to peer above the clouds,

80
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:55,020
but to conquer that space.

81
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:59,340
The idea of staying in space, of living in space,

82
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:01,640
is almost as old as the notion of being

83
00:05:01,740 --> 00:05:03,140
able to travel to space.

84
00:05:03,240 --> 00:05:06,040
I mean, most people didn't want to just go up and down.

85
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:08,240
They wanted to stay there when they got there.

86
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:13,580
Futurists have long dreamt of a space platform, which

87
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:19,020
could be used as a launch pad to travel to the moon or Mars,

88
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:22,220
a research facility, an observatory,

89
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:26,800
a colony for 100,000 people, or even a navigational guide

90
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:29,640
for ships.

91
00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:32,640
Edward Everett Hale wrote in 1869

92
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:35,500
a short story called The Brick Moon.

93
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:39,040
And in it, he depicts a group of people who

94
00:05:39,041 --> 00:05:43,480
who are launched into orbit essentially around the Earth

95
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:46,940
on a brick satellite.

96
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:48,980
Hale's brick moon was little more

97
00:05:49,080 --> 00:05:52,580
than a masonry capsule catapulted into orbit.

98
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:57,000
Visible to the naked eye, ships used it to set course.

99
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:59,600
People around the world could look into the sky

100
00:05:59,700 --> 00:06:02,300
and see the sphere.

101
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:05,200
For the first time, the dream of living in space

102
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:07,800
was set to paper.

103
00:06:07,801 --> 00:06:11,800
But just achieving space flight would take nearly 100 years

104
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:12,600
to accomplish.

105
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:19,180
With the birth of modern rocketry in the early 1900s,

106
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:21,520
the space station was one step closer

107
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:23,720
to becoming a reality.

108
00:06:23,820 --> 00:06:26,620
Scientists from the US, Germany, and Russia

109
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:29,960
wrote theoretically about rockets, space travel,

110
00:06:30,060 --> 00:06:33,200
and even living in space.

111
00:06:33,201 --> 00:06:38,340
One Austrian author wrote with almost prophetic accuracy.

112
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:40,800
Hermann Nordung's book published in 1929,

113
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:42,940
The Problem of Space Travel, describes

114
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:45,740
in great technical detail what is required

115
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:47,040
to build a space station.

116
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:50,080
He described all of the features that it would have.

117
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:52,180
It was going to be a large wheeled vehicle,

118
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:55,160
like we've seen repeatedly since that time,

119
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:57,560
that would be able to create artificial gravity.

120
00:06:57,660 --> 00:07:00,000
He had solar energy and solar arrays

121
00:07:00,001 --> 00:07:04,000
that would allow it to be powered without any other type

122
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:06,900
of electrical system in place.

123
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,940
And it was kind of the prototype of what we think of

124
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:13,240
when we think of a space station.

125
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:16,680
By rotating the large craft, centrifugal forces

126
00:07:16,780 --> 00:07:19,360
created the sensation of gravity.

127
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:21,480
Nordung, like many scientists, believed

128
00:07:21,580 --> 00:07:26,260
gravity was essential for long duration space flight.

129
00:07:26,261 --> 00:07:30,400
Although underappreciated in 1929, 23 years later,

130
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:33,500
rocket pioneer Werner von Braun borrowed

131
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:35,400
upon Nordung's vision.

132
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:38,500
By the early 1950s, von Braun was working

133
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:42,080
for the American space program and envisioned a space station

134
00:07:42,180 --> 00:07:45,740
like this as the logical step after sending a person into

135
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:48,280
orbit and before sending a person to the moon.

136
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:55,260
But von Braun's vision was still just a far off dream.

137
00:07:55,261 --> 00:07:58,000
for scientists in the United States and the Soviet Union

138
00:07:58,100 --> 00:08:01,000
were laboring to make manned space flight a reality.

139
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:06,940
Relations between the two superpowers

140
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:11,140
grew frigid at the dawn of the Cold War.

141
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:14,540
Fortunately, their competition would be played out high

142
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:16,940
above the battlefield rather than on it.

143
00:08:20,080 --> 00:08:23,080
The Great Space Race was just beginning to heat up.

144
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:28,360
The Soviet Union claimed the first victory.

145
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:31,740
Trepidation gripped the United States in 1957

146
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:35,140
when the Soviets successfully launched the first satellite,

147
00:08:35,240 --> 00:08:37,440
Sputnik, into orbit.

148
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:39,740
For the first time, stunned Americans

149
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:42,480
realized that not only did its Cold War enemy have

150
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:45,420
the nuclear bomb, the Soviet Union also

151
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:48,020
had the ability to deliver it.

152
00:08:48,120 --> 00:08:50,380
The same rocket that achieved orbit

153
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:53,080
could be pointed at the United States.

154
00:08:53,081 --> 00:08:55,520
I remember growing up as a child,

155
00:08:55,620 --> 00:08:58,520
and it was drilled into our head every day at school,

156
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:03,420
of the evil communists and the Russians, who were the bad people.

157
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:05,820
And they were just waiting to destroy us.

158
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:08,720
And we learned the old duck and cover routines

159
00:09:08,820 --> 00:09:10,340
of in case we heard an alarm go off,

160
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:14,340
we ducked under our desks in case of a nuclear attack.

161
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:19,880
Sundays, holidays, vacation time,

162
00:09:19,881 --> 00:09:22,880
We must be ready every day, all the time,

163
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:26,920
to do the right thing if the atomic bomb explodes.

164
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:29,420
Duck and cover.

165
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:31,820
This family knows what to do.

166
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:34,500
At the height of America's paranoia,

167
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:39,360
the Soviet Union triumphed again on April 12, 1961,

168
00:09:39,460 --> 00:09:43,340
by launching the first man into space, Yuri Gagarin.

169
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:53,120
Despite several successful US rocket launches,

170
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:56,920
embarrassing launch failures only worsened America's sinking

171
00:09:57,020 --> 00:09:57,780
public morale.

172
00:10:00,460 --> 00:10:02,760
President Kennedy needed something impressive

173
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:07,240
to boost spirits and prove America's superiority.

174
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:10,060
He looked to NASA for an answer.

175
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:13,740
Wernher von Braun suggested a bonafide space station,

176
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:16,780
different than the small, less capable capsules

177
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:19,040
NASA was launching.

178
00:10:19,041 --> 00:10:21,680
But von Braun's plans never took flight.

179
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:25,840
Kennedy instead opted for something a little more dramatic.

180
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:28,780
I believe that this nation should commit itself

181
00:10:28,880 --> 00:10:32,720
to achieving the goal before this decade is out

182
00:10:32,820 --> 00:10:35,860
of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely

183
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:37,260
to the Earth.

184
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:39,860
To meet President Kennedy's commitment,

185
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:43,400
a space station had to be postponed.

186
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:45,300
Beating the Soviet Union to the moon

187
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:47,600
became the only objective.

188
00:10:47,601 --> 00:10:51,300
It would take an astonishing amount of resources, technology,

189
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:55,180
development, and luck.

190
00:10:55,280 --> 00:10:58,180
Although the Soviet space program was in many ways

191
00:10:58,280 --> 00:11:01,080
more successful than the American program,

192
00:11:01,180 --> 00:11:04,120
the United States claimed the grand prize

193
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:09,260
by first landing a man on the moon in 1969.

194
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:13,660
He passed in the surface about one or two inches.

195
00:11:13,760 --> 00:11:17,600
Kennedy's objective was met.

196
00:11:17,601 --> 00:11:21,200
And I think the space race was the giant relief valve.

197
00:11:21,300 --> 00:11:24,400
We were not competing during the Cold War for ground

198
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:28,740
as much as we were competing for people's minds.

199
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:32,340
Russia conceded the moon to the United States

200
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:35,060
and put their efforts into accomplishing the dream

201
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:37,660
of the first space station.

202
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:40,560
The challenges were monumental.

203
00:11:40,660 --> 00:11:44,560
The platform in space wouldn't be won without tragedy.

204
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:52,600
Because scientists did not know if solid food was digestible

205
00:11:52,700 --> 00:11:55,600
in space, food for early space flights

206
00:11:55,700 --> 00:11:59,540
was pureed and put into toothpaste containers.

207
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:02,740
Space stations will return on Modern Marvels.

208
00:12:06,780 --> 00:12:12,760
We now return to space stations on Modern Marvels.

209
00:12:12,860 --> 00:12:17,260
Soviet engineers in Moscow faced overwhelming odds

210
00:12:17,261 --> 00:12:20,500
in the late 1960s, designing a spacecraft capable

211
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:24,260
of sustaining life for extended periods of time,

212
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:28,100
hundreds of miles above the Earth.

213
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:30,740
Orbiting the Earth once every 90 minutes,

214
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:33,240
the craft had to withstand extreme temperature

215
00:12:33,340 --> 00:12:35,640
fluctuations of 500 degrees.

216
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:41,380
The craft had to be self-contained

217
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:45,160
and required more sophisticated life support systems,

218
00:12:45,161 --> 00:12:49,760
power generation, waste removal, and oxygen purification

219
00:12:49,860 --> 00:12:51,740
than on any previous craft.

220
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:58,200
As engineers worked to overcome the technical obstacles,

221
00:12:58,300 --> 00:13:01,240
physicians questioned how lengthy space travel

222
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:04,880
would affect the human body.

223
00:13:04,980 --> 00:13:09,320
Would cosmonauts suffer digestive problems, muscle loss,

224
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:12,920
or even psychological effects?

225
00:13:12,921 --> 00:13:16,260
The Soviet Union planned to answer all of these questions.

226
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:23,660
In the late 1960s, Russia intensified efforts

227
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:25,660
to build the first space station.

228
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:30,700
They immediately got the world's first space station,

229
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:34,180
up Solute 1, which was initially a great success.

230
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:37,720
On April 19, 1971, a Russian rocket

231
00:13:37,820 --> 00:13:41,540
lifted off the launch pad in Bakunor, Kazakhstan,

232
00:13:41,541 --> 00:13:45,480
carrying the first space station, Salyut 1.

233
00:13:45,580 --> 00:13:48,120
The US, concentrating on the moon,

234
00:13:48,220 --> 00:13:50,280
didn't have a space station ready,

235
00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:54,280
and conceded that milestone to the Russians.

236
00:13:54,380 --> 00:13:57,060
Manned by a three-cosmonaut crew,

237
00:13:57,160 --> 00:14:01,420
Salyut 1 looked nothing like the popular wheel space station

238
00:14:01,520 --> 00:14:03,060
concept.

239
00:14:03,160 --> 00:14:05,660
It didn't produce artificial gravity,

240
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:09,300
and it didn't have a lot of workspace.

241
00:14:09,301 --> 00:14:12,800
But the cramped quarters about the size of a school bus

242
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:15,840
were still larger than any previous space vehicle.

243
00:14:19,540 --> 00:14:22,580
The 23-day mission of experiments and observations

244
00:14:22,680 --> 00:14:24,820
went nearly perfectly.

245
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:27,920
Then, tragedy.

246
00:14:28,020 --> 00:14:31,560
During the return to Earth, their Soyuz transport craft

247
00:14:31,660 --> 00:14:35,820
leaked life-giving oxygen into the vacuum of space.

248
00:14:35,821 --> 00:14:39,260
All three cosmonauts suffocated as they re-entered

249
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:41,820
the Earth's atmosphere.

250
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:46,660
Paramedics tried in vain to resuscitate the three.

251
00:14:46,760 --> 00:14:51,160
Russia mourned the loss of their heroes.

252
00:14:51,260 --> 00:14:54,480
The event underscored the hazards of space flight.

253
00:14:57,640 --> 00:15:01,280
Although it ended in tragedy, Salyut-1 space station

254
00:15:01,380 --> 00:15:03,740
marked the beginning of an impressive history

255
00:15:03,741 --> 00:15:05,640
of Russians living in space.

256
00:15:10,140 --> 00:15:13,780
But the Americans weren't far behind.

257
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:16,480
Delayed by their goal of reaching the moon,

258
00:15:16,580 --> 00:15:19,080
they wouldn't have the first station,

259
00:15:19,180 --> 00:15:22,420
but they pledged to have the best.

260
00:15:22,520 --> 00:15:26,560
Beginning in 1966, NASA developed what was referred to

261
00:15:26,660 --> 00:15:30,500
as the Apollo Applications Program, which became Skylab.

262
00:15:30,600 --> 00:15:33,200
It took some of the technology and one

263
00:15:33,201 --> 00:15:37,100
and one of the upper stages from the Saturn V spacecraft

264
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:41,380
reconfigured it into a long cylindrical space station,

265
00:15:41,480 --> 00:15:45,580
essentially, a space lab, which we called Skylab.

266
00:15:45,680 --> 00:15:48,480
The 118-foot-long American station

267
00:15:48,580 --> 00:15:51,580
was outfitted with living quarters, telescopes,

268
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:55,620
and scientific experiments, making it far larger

269
00:15:55,720 --> 00:16:00,160
and more sophisticated than the Soviet station.

270
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:01,260
8.

271
00:16:01,261 --> 00:16:03,220
We have ignition sequence has started.

272
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:08,500
On May 14, 1973, two years after the Salyut launch,

273
00:16:08,600 --> 00:16:15,500
Skylab blasted unmanned into the sky atop a Saturn V rocket.

274
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:19,040
It was a brilliant launch, but immediately problems

275
00:16:19,140 --> 00:16:21,740
developed.

276
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:25,360
During the launch, Skylab lost a meteoroid shield that also

277
00:16:25,460 --> 00:16:27,620
acted to shade the station.

278
00:16:27,720 --> 00:16:29,980
At ground control, panicked engineers

279
00:16:29,981 --> 00:16:32,780
watched helplessly as temperatures quickly rose

280
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:37,420
inside the station to dangerous levels.

281
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:40,760
More devastating news came when ground controllers realized

282
00:16:40,860 --> 00:16:44,100
one of the solar arrays that provided power to the station

283
00:16:44,200 --> 00:16:46,160
had ripped off during the launch,

284
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:49,940
and another array did not fully deploy.

285
00:16:50,040 --> 00:16:54,300
Without power, Skylab risked overheating or freezing,

286
00:16:54,400 --> 00:16:57,240
dooming the $2.5 billion station.

287
00:16:59,980 --> 00:17:04,380
NASA engineers worked day and night to find a solution.

288
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:07,120
The first crew scheduled to be launched separately

289
00:17:07,220 --> 00:17:09,520
and rendezvous with the station was delayed

290
00:17:09,620 --> 00:17:14,600
and given a crash course and Skylab repair.

291
00:17:14,700 --> 00:17:17,200
After 10 days of intense training,

292
00:17:17,300 --> 00:17:21,440
astronauts Charles Conrad, Joseph Kirwin, and Paul Weitz

293
00:17:21,540 --> 00:17:25,080
were launched into orbit and positioned their Apollo capsule

294
00:17:25,180 --> 00:17:26,780
to dock with the crippled station.

295
00:17:29,980 --> 00:17:34,620
Inside Skylab, the temperature soared to 130 degrees Fahrenheit.

296
00:17:37,920 --> 00:17:40,120
A series of dangerous spacewalks

297
00:17:40,220 --> 00:17:43,860
freed the stuck solar array, and a sunshade placed

298
00:17:43,960 --> 00:17:45,900
over the exterior of the station

299
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,840
slowly cooled interior temperatures

300
00:17:48,940 --> 00:17:51,160
to livable conditions.

301
00:17:51,260 --> 00:17:54,700
The quick response saved the station.

302
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:57,540
Triumphant, the first Skylab crew

303
00:17:57,541 --> 00:18:00,240
settled into their new weightless home.

304
00:18:00,340 --> 00:18:04,240
Now, the astronauts could do all that fun stuff

305
00:18:04,340 --> 00:18:06,880
the rest of Earth could only dream about.

306
00:18:06,980 --> 00:18:09,780
Besides providing a cosmic playhouse,

307
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:12,380
the near weightless environment on Skylab

308
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:16,900
offered the opportunity to study materials in a unique way.

309
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,200
Gravity, we don't realize how it affects

310
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:22,200
so many aspects of our life.

311
00:18:22,300 --> 00:18:24,940
There are certain medicines that are

312
00:18:24,941 --> 00:18:27,340
are impossible to do down here on the Earth.

313
00:18:27,440 --> 00:18:29,740
But up in space, they're able to crystallize

314
00:18:29,840 --> 00:18:33,640
some of these medications in very much, much stronger

315
00:18:33,740 --> 00:18:35,780
crystals, much more perfect crystals,

316
00:18:35,880 --> 00:18:39,280
and enable some things to be done that they can't do down here.

317
00:18:39,380 --> 00:18:42,200
During the three missions aboard Skylab,

318
00:18:42,300 --> 00:18:45,200
astronauts also performed countless experiments

319
00:18:45,300 --> 00:18:48,060
on the effects of weightlessness on the body.

320
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:52,060
For years, scientists knew the low impact environment in space

321
00:18:52,160 --> 00:18:54,240
caused muscles to atrophy.

322
00:18:54,241 --> 00:18:58,380
the Americans used various exercise routines and equipment

323
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:02,580
to counteract muscle shrinkage.

324
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:05,120
The Russians also developed exercise equipment

325
00:19:05,220 --> 00:19:07,360
for extended periods of weightlessness

326
00:19:07,460 --> 00:19:09,960
aboard a space station.

327
00:19:10,060 --> 00:19:14,460
Engineers also created a highly advanced and extraordinary

328
00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:18,400
semi-closed environment aboard Skylab.

329
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:21,640
Because of the limiting factors on a space station,

330
00:19:21,641 --> 00:19:24,340
Finite resources such as air and water

331
00:19:24,440 --> 00:19:27,120
are valuable and preferably recyclable.

332
00:19:30,020 --> 00:19:34,180
Skylab's environmental control included a filtration and odor

333
00:19:34,280 --> 00:19:37,460
removal system that scrubbed the oxygen,

334
00:19:37,560 --> 00:19:40,700
removing carbon dioxide and odors.

335
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:43,960
Carbon air circulated over beds of zeolite,

336
00:19:44,060 --> 00:19:46,300
naturally occurring silicate minerals,

337
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:51,640
which acted as molecular sieves trapping CO2 molecules.

338
00:19:51,641 --> 00:19:55,280
The semi-closed system also included a sophisticated waste

339
00:19:55,380 --> 00:19:57,080
management process.

340
00:19:57,180 --> 00:19:58,640
Still today, after all these years,

341
00:19:58,740 --> 00:20:00,980
the number one question that astronauts are asked

342
00:20:01,080 --> 00:20:02,780
is how do they use the bathroom in space?

343
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:05,480
I think it's probably best summarized by an old quote

344
00:20:05,580 --> 00:20:08,220
by Apollo 9 astronaut Rusty Swigert.

345
00:20:08,320 --> 00:20:11,000
He just said, there ain't no graceful way.

346
00:20:11,100 --> 00:20:14,300
In the absence of gravity, the station instead

347
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:16,500
used a suction system.

348
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:20,500
Skylab's state-of-the-art toilet came complete with seat belt

349
00:20:20,501 --> 00:20:22,300
and foot straps.

350
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:25,740
No one wanted to risk accidentally leaving the scene

351
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:27,040
at a crucial moment.

352
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:32,720
Bodily excretions were automatically collected,

353
00:20:32,820 --> 00:20:35,440
separated, and packaged for study back on Earth.

354
00:20:38,160 --> 00:20:44,000
On July 11, 1979, gravity finally pulled the unmanned

355
00:20:44,100 --> 00:20:49,060
station into the atmosphere where it burned up.

356
00:20:49,061 --> 00:20:50,600
I think there was a general feeling

357
00:20:50,700 --> 00:20:54,360
that it was a real tragedy that we actually had to let it

358
00:20:54,460 --> 00:20:55,860
reenter.

359
00:20:55,960 --> 00:21:00,080
Of course, it was defined as a finite program,

360
00:21:00,180 --> 00:21:03,240
but we had an opportunity at one point to reboost it.

361
00:21:03,340 --> 00:21:05,240
It wasn't funded.

362
00:21:05,340 --> 00:21:08,120
The station performed admirably.

363
00:21:08,220 --> 00:21:11,040
Three crews served aboard Skyland.

364
00:21:11,140 --> 00:21:14,580
The last returned February 8, 1974,

365
00:21:14,581 --> 00:21:17,920
after a world record 84 days in space.

366
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:23,820
Skylab's last crew temporarily beat the Russian duration

367
00:21:23,920 --> 00:21:25,520
record for living in space.

368
00:21:25,620 --> 00:21:27,720
But that wouldn't last long.

369
00:21:27,820 --> 00:21:30,900
As America worked on a reusable space shuttle,

370
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:34,760
Russia intensified its space station program.

371
00:21:34,860 --> 00:21:38,140
The biggest and most impressive station

372
00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:40,740
was on the horizon.

373
00:21:40,840 --> 00:21:43,100
The space shuttle was initially intended

374
00:21:43,101 --> 00:21:47,340
to be a ferrying vehicle between the Earth and a space station

375
00:21:47,440 --> 00:21:49,940
to be built at a later date.

376
00:21:50,040 --> 00:21:53,040
Space stations will return on Modern Marvels.

377
00:21:57,840 --> 00:22:02,820
We now return to Space Stations on Modern Marvels.

378
00:22:02,920 --> 00:22:06,920
The early 1970s brought the birth of the space station.

379
00:22:07,020 --> 00:22:10,200
Both Russia and America launched stations,

380
00:22:10,201 --> 00:22:13,440
but they weren't equally committed to their programs.

381
00:22:13,540 --> 00:22:16,400
Skylab was a short-term project and abandoned

382
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:20,640
after only three missions due to a lack of funding.

383
00:22:20,740 --> 00:22:22,840
The Soviets, on the other hand, launched

384
00:22:22,940 --> 00:22:27,320
six additional Salyut stations during the 1970s.

385
00:22:27,420 --> 00:22:30,120
They planned to stay in space.

386
00:22:30,220 --> 00:22:34,220
America was just visiting.

387
00:22:34,320 --> 00:22:37,360
Even though we went up with a little spirit of competition

388
00:22:37,460 --> 00:22:38,860
with the Russians, it didn't last long.

389
00:22:38,861 --> 00:22:41,700
And the Russians just sat there and kept plugging along at it

390
00:22:41,800 --> 00:22:44,760
and just consistently working at learning

391
00:22:44,860 --> 00:22:47,200
what a space station is all about and how to build them

392
00:22:47,300 --> 00:22:48,900
and how to keep them up there.

393
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:52,040
During the 1970s, the Soviet Union

394
00:22:52,140 --> 00:22:54,600
stepped up their space station program,

395
00:22:54,700 --> 00:22:57,920
continually improving their many stations.

396
00:22:58,020 --> 00:23:01,380
The US planned to build an even more impressive station

397
00:23:01,480 --> 00:23:03,220
after Skylab.

398
00:23:03,320 --> 00:23:06,620
But first, NASA decided on an intermediate step,

399
00:23:06,720 --> 00:23:08,020
the space shuttle.

400
00:23:08,021 --> 00:23:09,680
One.

401
00:23:09,780 --> 00:23:12,220
And we have liftoff of the space shuttle Columbia

402
00:23:12,320 --> 00:23:17,160
on an international life science and microgravity mission.

403
00:23:17,260 --> 00:23:20,460
Houston now controlling the flight of Columbia.

404
00:23:20,560 --> 00:23:23,160
The shuttle provided a research environment

405
00:23:23,260 --> 00:23:26,380
similar to a space station.

406
00:23:26,480 --> 00:23:29,340
Aboard the shuttle, astronauts conducted experiments

407
00:23:29,440 --> 00:23:38,020
on plants, animals, insects, and even fire.

408
00:23:38,021 --> 00:23:42,020
Although shuttle flights provided exceptional research,

409
00:23:42,120 --> 00:23:45,420
the shuttle had its drawbacks.

410
00:23:45,520 --> 00:23:48,360
It was relatively cramped, and shuttle systems

411
00:23:48,460 --> 00:23:51,960
weren't designed for long durations in space.

412
00:23:52,060 --> 00:23:54,060
The shuttle is a wonderful vehicle,

413
00:23:54,160 --> 00:23:56,180
but it could stay in orbit only two weeks.

414
00:23:56,280 --> 00:23:58,180
It's kind of an extended camping trip,

415
00:23:58,280 --> 00:24:01,780
and no way is it a space station.

416
00:24:01,880 --> 00:24:05,120
Having the same basic needs as a space station,

417
00:24:05,220 --> 00:24:07,680
but with less room, the shuttle could only

418
00:24:07,681 --> 00:24:11,240
could only carry enough food, water, and fuel cell materials

419
00:24:11,241 --> 00:24:13,740
to support life approximately two weeks.

420
00:24:16,820 --> 00:24:20,100
But NASA made the shuttle into an all-purpose vehicle

421
00:24:20,101 --> 00:24:22,200
with great success.

422
00:24:22,201 --> 00:24:26,940
Still, NASA longed for another project.

423
00:24:26,941 --> 00:24:31,860
Then came the announcement on January 25, 1984,

424
00:24:31,861 --> 00:24:36,280
a long-awaited call resonating back to the early days of NASA

425
00:24:36,281 --> 00:24:39,220
when von Braun and other scientists dreamed of a space

426
00:24:39,221 --> 00:24:42,520
platform of superior capabilities.

427
00:24:42,521 --> 00:24:45,660
Tonight, I am directing NASA to develop a permanently manned

428
00:24:45,661 --> 00:24:48,400
space station and to do it within a decade.

429
00:24:51,760 --> 00:24:54,540
We want our friends to help us meet these challenges

430
00:24:54,541 --> 00:24:56,240
and share in their benefits.

431
00:24:56,241 --> 00:24:59,140
NASA will invite other countries to participate

432
00:24:59,141 --> 00:25:01,880
so we can strengthen peace, build prosperity,

433
00:25:01,881 --> 00:25:05,840
and expand freedom for all who share our goals.

434
00:25:05,841 --> 00:25:08,740
Reminiscent of Kennedy's bold declaration,

435
00:25:08,741 --> 00:25:12,020
Reagan introduced plans for an international space station

436
00:25:12,021 --> 00:25:13,940
named Freedom.

437
00:25:13,941 --> 00:25:17,480
The collaborative effort also promised a far larger, more

438
00:25:17,481 --> 00:25:21,680
capable platform than ever before.

439
00:25:21,681 --> 00:25:25,260
But the Russians didn't care to participate.

440
00:25:25,261 --> 00:25:28,120
They had more immediate plans.

441
00:25:28,121 --> 00:25:31,940
In February 1986, the next generation of Russian space

442
00:25:31,941 --> 00:25:34,340
obtained orbit, the Mir.

443
00:25:37,740 --> 00:25:42,680
Meaning peace in Russian, the Mir was like no previous station.

444
00:25:42,780 --> 00:25:45,520
It was expandable.

445
00:25:45,620 --> 00:25:48,020
Over the next several years, Russians

446
00:25:48,120 --> 00:25:52,020
launched additional modules to increase the size of the station

447
00:25:52,120 --> 00:25:54,800
to 120 tons.

448
00:25:54,900 --> 00:25:57,700
Modules automatically snapped together.

449
00:26:01,940 --> 00:26:04,880
The Russians commuted between the ground and the station

450
00:26:04,980 --> 00:26:08,040
in the same tried and true Soyuz capsules

451
00:26:08,140 --> 00:26:10,740
that they'd been using for the past 15 years.

452
00:26:13,820 --> 00:26:16,460
Once docked, the Soyuz vehicles also

453
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:21,500
served as a scapecraft in case of an emergency.

454
00:26:21,600 --> 00:26:25,660
Supplies were sent separately on unmanned Progress supply

455
00:26:25,760 --> 00:26:27,960
vessels.

456
00:26:28,060 --> 00:26:29,540
A tremendous amount of science has

457
00:26:29,541 --> 00:26:32,080
science has been conducted aboard Mir.

458
00:26:32,081 --> 00:26:36,200
It has given the Russians the absolute perfect example

459
00:26:36,201 --> 00:26:38,120
of what an orbiting space station should be.

460
00:26:38,121 --> 00:26:41,440
It was a very stable platform that orbited the Earth.

461
00:26:41,441 --> 00:26:44,300
They were able to send crews up and down very easily.

462
00:26:44,301 --> 00:26:45,920
They had crews that lived on board

463
00:26:45,921 --> 00:26:47,600
for more than a year at a time.

464
00:26:47,601 --> 00:26:50,360
The Russians have accumulated four to five times

465
00:26:50,361 --> 00:26:54,200
the amount of man hours in space than the United States has.

466
00:26:54,201 --> 00:26:56,580
And it's simply because they've always maintained

467
00:26:56,581 --> 00:26:58,400
a manned presence aboard that space station,

468
00:26:58,401 --> 00:27:01,600
which is phenomenal in itself.

469
00:27:01,601 --> 00:27:05,740
The Mir included several complex life support systems

470
00:27:05,741 --> 00:27:08,580
designed for the five to seven year lifespan

471
00:27:08,581 --> 00:27:10,080
of the space station.

472
00:27:10,081 --> 00:27:12,740
But most hardware was functionally simple.

473
00:27:12,741 --> 00:27:15,620
There's vast differences of how the Americans and Russians

474
00:27:15,621 --> 00:27:19,960
approach the same problem and challenge in space.

475
00:27:19,961 --> 00:27:22,660
The Russians have always designed things the simplest way

476
00:27:22,661 --> 00:27:23,560
they could.

477
00:27:23,561 --> 00:27:25,600
Then if it worked, they've never changed it.

478
00:27:25,601 --> 00:27:28,060
Whereas the Americans come along, and they always

479
00:27:28,061 --> 00:27:31,360
the envelope of technology, make things so sophisticated

480
00:27:31,460 --> 00:27:33,300
they supposedly can't break down.

481
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:36,200
Then if it works, see if we can make it better.

482
00:27:36,300 --> 00:27:38,700
But things did break down.

483
00:27:38,800 --> 00:27:43,440
On January 28, 1986, the space shuttle Challenger

484
00:27:43,540 --> 00:27:49,040
exploded just after takeoff, killing seven astronauts.

485
00:27:49,140 --> 00:27:52,720
It was the worst US space disaster in history.

486
00:27:52,820 --> 00:27:56,920
It also nearly doomed space station freedom.

487
00:27:56,921 --> 00:28:00,760
In addition, cost overruns and smaller than predicted budgets

488
00:28:00,860 --> 00:28:02,860
delayed the station.

489
00:28:02,960 --> 00:28:06,760
Reagan's commitment for a completed station within a decade

490
00:28:06,860 --> 00:28:08,940
could not be met.

491
00:28:09,040 --> 00:28:14,140
The fledgling ISS faced termination.

492
00:28:14,240 --> 00:28:17,400
Space research has provided such consumer products

493
00:28:17,500 --> 00:28:22,680
as cell phones, cordless power tools, and Mylar food packaging.

494
00:28:22,780 --> 00:28:26,020
Space stations will return on Modern Marvels.

495
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:31,920
We now return to space stations on Modern Marvels.

496
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:37,660
The 1990s brought world change.

497
00:28:37,760 --> 00:28:41,540
The US started rebuilding relations with Russia

498
00:28:41,640 --> 00:28:44,300
and became involved in the largest military conflict

499
00:28:44,400 --> 00:28:46,400
since Vietnam.

500
00:28:46,500 --> 00:28:49,440
The greatest threat to NASA's space station, however,

501
00:28:49,540 --> 00:28:51,740
came from within.

502
00:28:51,840 --> 00:28:56,920
The US had already spent $8 billion on the ISS.

503
00:28:56,921 --> 00:29:01,360
which threatened to climb to 31 billion before completion.

504
00:29:01,460 --> 00:29:05,420
Newly elected President Clinton mandated a leaner NASA.

505
00:29:05,520 --> 00:29:07,800
Reluctantly, NASA redesigned the station,

506
00:29:07,900 --> 00:29:12,700
creating this smaller, restricted version of freedom.

507
00:29:12,800 --> 00:29:15,980
Then came the most significant development in the history

508
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:20,480
of US-Russia space relations.

509
00:29:20,580 --> 00:29:23,620
President Clinton invited the Russians once again

510
00:29:23,720 --> 00:29:26,920
aboard the International Space Station.

511
00:29:26,921 --> 00:29:31,680
A group of advocates both inside and outside the government developed with the notion that

512
00:29:31,681 --> 00:29:39,400
Russia could bring lots of experience, lots of capability to the space station program,

513
00:29:39,401 --> 00:29:45,600
plus engaging Russia with the space station would provide something for very talented

514
00:29:45,601 --> 00:29:51,040
technical people in Russia to do rather than selling their services to the Iraqs or Libyas

515
00:29:51,041 --> 00:29:52,640
or North Koreas of the world.

516
00:29:52,641 --> 00:29:54,800
So it was a strategic choice, ultimately,

517
00:29:54,900 --> 00:29:59,340
by the Clinton administration, both for space program reasons

518
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:03,020
and broader security reasons, to invite Russia in.

519
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:07,660
Russia gladly accepted the offer and the $400 million

520
00:30:07,760 --> 00:30:11,520
the US pledged to pay for the experience only the Russians

521
00:30:11,620 --> 00:30:13,820
could provide.

522
00:30:13,920 --> 00:30:16,700
The Shuttle Mir, or Phase I program,

523
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:19,540
would be the first step before actually building

524
00:30:19,640 --> 00:30:22,200
the International Space Station.

525
00:30:22,201 --> 00:30:25,200
Phase one included docking the shuttle and Mir

526
00:30:25,300 --> 00:30:27,600
and sending several American astronauts

527
00:30:27,700 --> 00:30:30,740
to live aboard the station.

528
00:30:30,840 --> 00:30:33,540
With Russia's entrance into the ISS program

529
00:30:33,640 --> 00:30:37,740
and the public's growing support, the pared down price tag

530
00:30:37,840 --> 00:30:40,760
began to grow again.

531
00:30:40,860 --> 00:30:43,720
After more than a year of training in Russia,

532
00:30:43,820 --> 00:30:47,760
astronaut Norman Thaggard rode a Russian Soyuz rocket

533
00:30:47,860 --> 00:30:51,000
to become the first American aboard the Mir.

534
00:30:51,001 --> 00:30:54,940
It was the dawn of a new era of international cooperation

535
00:30:55,040 --> 00:30:58,040
in space.

536
00:30:58,140 --> 00:31:02,020
Norman Thaggard spent three months aboard the Mir doing research

537
00:31:02,120 --> 00:31:05,280
and learning about living in space.

538
00:31:05,380 --> 00:31:10,680
Then on June 29, 1995, the shuttle docked with the Mir

539
00:31:10,780 --> 00:31:16,960
for the first time to take Thaggard home and make history.

540
00:31:17,060 --> 00:31:20,320
For five days, both Mir and shuttle crews

541
00:31:20,321 --> 00:31:22,020
together.

542
00:31:22,120 --> 00:31:25,820
Fortunate astronauts who took part in the historic event

543
00:31:25,920 --> 00:31:29,120
didn't know what to expect.

544
00:31:29,220 --> 00:31:32,860
It was kind of like a lived in an apartment.

545
00:31:32,960 --> 00:31:35,640
And of course, the Mir didn't have the capability

546
00:31:35,740 --> 00:31:39,500
to send hardware and cargo back down to the ground.

547
00:31:39,600 --> 00:31:44,580
So they'd been accumulating all of this interesting stuff

548
00:31:44,680 --> 00:31:46,740
for a decade.

549
00:31:46,840 --> 00:31:49,020
So there were experiments on experiments

550
00:31:49,021 --> 00:31:51,560
strapped by bungee cords to the walls,

551
00:31:51,660 --> 00:31:55,120
but at the same time, very roomy.

552
00:31:55,220 --> 00:31:59,860
Thaggert returned to Earth with an American endurance record.

553
00:31:59,960 --> 00:32:02,240
Following Thaggert's landmark adventure,

554
00:32:02,340 --> 00:32:05,940
astronaut Shannon Lucid captured headlines.

555
00:32:06,040 --> 00:32:08,400
After spending four months in space,

556
00:32:08,500 --> 00:32:11,540
her shuttle return flight was delayed.

557
00:32:11,640 --> 00:32:15,080
Lucid's tour aboard Mir stretched to six months,

558
00:32:15,180 --> 00:32:18,380
a new American endurance record.

559
00:32:18,381 --> 00:32:22,280
Taking it in stride, Lucid only hoped to be home in time

560
00:32:22,380 --> 00:32:24,480
for Christmas.

561
00:32:24,580 --> 00:32:26,680
Lucid got her wish.

562
00:32:26,780 --> 00:32:29,760
The president and the American public welcomed her back

563
00:32:29,860 --> 00:32:37,860
to Earth with open arms and a bag of M&Ms, her favorite candy.

564
00:32:37,960 --> 00:32:39,660
Soon after returning to Earth, she

565
00:32:39,760 --> 00:32:43,880
said she couldn't wait to get back into space.

566
00:32:43,980 --> 00:32:46,200
The next two astronauts to live aboard Mir

567
00:32:46,201 --> 00:32:49,540
didn't have the same enjoyable experience.

568
00:32:49,640 --> 00:32:56,280
John Blaha and Jerry Lininger found the isolation of space,

569
00:32:56,380 --> 00:32:59,240
the ever-increasing system breakdowns,

570
00:32:59,340 --> 00:33:02,380
and personality conflicts between crewmates

571
00:33:02,480 --> 00:33:04,980
exceedingly distressing.

572
00:33:05,080 --> 00:33:06,780
There are large cultural differences

573
00:33:06,880 --> 00:33:08,620
between our country and theirs.

574
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:14,760
And the crews trained together and lived together as much

575
00:33:14,761 --> 00:33:15,960
as they could on the ground.

576
00:33:16,060 --> 00:33:21,160
But you don't erase an individual's 40 years of upbringing

577
00:33:21,260 --> 00:33:23,860
in a couple of months of working together.

578
00:33:23,960 --> 00:33:26,400
But the culture clash proved to be the least

579
00:33:26,500 --> 00:33:30,380
of their concerns when Jerry Lininger faced a life and death

580
00:33:30,480 --> 00:33:32,380
situation aboard Mir.

581
00:33:32,480 --> 00:33:33,880
The youngest cosmonaut's host,

582
00:33:33,980 --> 00:33:37,080
Luzukin, goes to add what they call a candle, which

583
00:33:37,180 --> 00:33:41,060
is a bad name for an oxygen canister that they use

584
00:33:41,160 --> 00:33:44,360
to add oxygen when there's extra people on board.

585
00:33:44,361 --> 00:33:47,360
For some reason that night, for a reason that to this moment,

586
00:33:47,460 --> 00:33:49,860
so far as I know, no one has ever explained,

587
00:33:49,960 --> 00:33:53,800
the candle, in fact, the entire apparatus,

588
00:33:53,900 --> 00:33:57,500
burst into flame like a blowtorch out right in front of

589
00:33:57,600 --> 00:33:59,340
Luzutkin who goes like this.

590
00:33:59,440 --> 00:34:02,520
And it's a blowtorch like 18 inches that steadily grows

591
00:34:02,620 --> 00:34:04,040
and grows and grows.

592
00:34:04,140 --> 00:34:06,080
And everyone starts yelling, Pajar, Pajar,

593
00:34:06,180 --> 00:34:07,820
which is Russian for fire.

594
00:34:07,920 --> 00:34:10,260
Larry Korzun, the commander at that time,

595
00:34:10,360 --> 00:34:13,420
shoots in, bodily extracts Luzutkin,

596
00:34:13,421 --> 00:34:16,160
and throws them out and says, you know, get out of the way.

597
00:34:16,260 --> 00:34:17,760
I'm going to try to put this out.

598
00:34:17,860 --> 00:34:20,020
And he knows that if he doesn't put it out soon,

599
00:34:20,520 --> 00:34:22,300
it'll either reach the other side of the hole

600
00:34:22,301 --> 00:34:27,200
or it could fall to some part of the hole, burn a hole in the hole,

601
00:34:27,340 --> 00:34:29,780
and they lose all their oxygen supply like that.

602
00:34:30,300 --> 00:34:33,380
They manage to get a series of fire extinguishers out,

603
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:34,660
hand them to Corzoon.

604
00:34:34,860 --> 00:34:35,900
One runs out.

605
00:34:35,980 --> 00:34:36,820
He throws it aside.

606
00:34:36,920 --> 00:34:38,720
He gets another, throws it aside.

607
00:34:38,721 --> 00:34:42,080
Finally, he decides to switch from shooting foam on it

608
00:34:42,081 --> 00:34:43,820
shooting water onto it.

609
00:34:43,920 --> 00:34:47,060
And he notices that eventually the white shining area

610
00:34:47,160 --> 00:34:49,420
seems to be shrinking and shrinking and shrinking

611
00:34:49,520 --> 00:34:53,120
until somewhat, anticlimactically, it goes out.

612
00:34:53,220 --> 00:34:55,920
And everybody kind of goes, we're still alive.

613
00:34:56,020 --> 00:34:57,620
We're still alive.

614
00:34:57,720 --> 00:35:00,460
Already in orbit longer than anticipated,

615
00:35:00,560 --> 00:35:03,060
the mirror was getting a bad reputation.

616
00:35:03,160 --> 00:35:05,400
Word spread about other system problems,

617
00:35:05,500 --> 00:35:08,840
including testy computers and coolant leaks

618
00:35:08,940 --> 00:35:12,080
that oozed large antifreeze globules into the cabin.

619
00:35:12,081 --> 00:35:16,520
Jerry Leninger returned to Earth after 132 days

620
00:35:16,620 --> 00:35:20,120
and urged NASA to take another look at the soundness of phase

621
00:35:20,220 --> 00:35:21,220
one.

622
00:35:21,320 --> 00:35:24,860
But NASA, confident in the program and the integrity

623
00:35:24,960 --> 00:35:28,400
of the Mir, sent up the fourth astronaut, Mike Foll.

624
00:35:32,500 --> 00:35:36,200
Foll enjoyed working with his cosmonaut crewmates,

625
00:35:36,300 --> 00:35:40,200
Vasily Sableyev and Alexander Lesutkin.

626
00:35:40,201 --> 00:35:43,800
Both Russians had been aboard when the fire erupted.

627
00:35:43,900 --> 00:35:46,900
Now they spent most of their time making repairs

628
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:48,100
to the aging station.

629
00:35:50,500 --> 00:35:54,740
Public concern grew over the minor problems on Mir.

630
00:35:54,840 --> 00:35:57,520
But another near catastrophic event

631
00:35:57,620 --> 00:36:01,960
would shake NASA's faith in the phase one program.

632
00:36:02,060 --> 00:36:06,220
On June 25, 1997, during a training exercise,

633
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:09,300
the Russian commander Vasily Sobleev

634
00:36:09,301 --> 00:36:13,440
Liev attempted to dock an unmanned progress resupply

635
00:36:13,441 --> 00:36:15,780
vessel by remote control.

636
00:36:15,781 --> 00:36:18,180
They wanted him to do it by sight, to estimate,

637
00:36:18,181 --> 00:36:22,480
to guesstimate with a stopwatch and a laser rangefinder how fast

638
00:36:22,481 --> 00:36:23,640
it was coming.

639
00:36:23,641 --> 00:36:25,420
Well, the fact was, after all he'd been through,

640
00:36:25,421 --> 00:36:29,160
after a fire that they'd had, the near collision that they had,

641
00:36:29,161 --> 00:36:31,060
he was not real good shape.

642
00:36:31,061 --> 00:36:34,060
They sent it in on what's called a very hot trajectory, very

643
00:36:34,061 --> 00:36:37,700
steep, very fast, and through a combination of human

644
00:36:37,701 --> 00:36:39,540
and systemic errors.

645
00:36:39,541 --> 00:36:40,700
He just couldn't handle it.

646
00:36:40,701 --> 00:36:41,820
It came in too fast.

647
00:36:41,821 --> 00:36:44,540
By the time he started breaking, it was too late.

648
00:36:44,541 --> 00:36:46,940
And he began shouting again, get to the Soyuz,

649
00:36:46,941 --> 00:36:49,000
get to the Soyuz, we have to evacuate.

650
00:36:49,001 --> 00:36:51,160
And then it hit.

651
00:36:51,161 --> 00:36:52,640
When the progress hit the station,

652
00:36:52,641 --> 00:36:56,180
Mike Fole and Sasha Luzutkin both scrambled into action

653
00:36:56,181 --> 00:36:59,540
and quickly realized that they had a hole in the side

654
00:36:59,541 --> 00:37:01,480
of the station, a decompression, and that they

655
00:37:01,481 --> 00:37:05,680
had to isolate or seal off that area of the station

656
00:37:05,681 --> 00:37:07,720
so that they wouldn't lose the air.

657
00:37:07,721 --> 00:37:10,280
The vacuum of space was sucking the oxygen

658
00:37:10,281 --> 00:37:11,420
from the station.

659
00:37:11,421 --> 00:37:14,680
Foll and Lazutkin managed to disconnect cables running

660
00:37:14,681 --> 00:37:18,460
between modules and close the hatch.

661
00:37:18,461 --> 00:37:24,140
The crew's quick response saved their lives and the station.

662
00:37:24,141 --> 00:37:26,300
After the incident, many Americans

663
00:37:26,301 --> 00:37:31,180
demanded Foll be removed from the decaying station.

664
00:37:31,181 --> 00:37:33,140
Americans want to call it a death trap

665
00:37:33,141 --> 00:37:35,580
because they've never encountered anything like that.

666
00:37:35,581 --> 00:37:38,080
and because they've never had a space station before.

667
00:37:38,081 --> 00:37:39,760
Americans don't understand the fact

668
00:37:39,761 --> 00:37:42,220
that when you put a chunk of metal up in space

669
00:37:42,221 --> 00:37:46,020
and you put men on it for months and years, things break down.

670
00:37:46,021 --> 00:37:49,060
We had lengthy discussions with the Russians,

671
00:37:49,061 --> 00:37:50,760
with our superiors within NASA,

672
00:37:50,761 --> 00:37:52,740
and consulted with members of Congress.

673
00:37:52,741 --> 00:37:54,240
And we made it very clear that safety

674
00:37:54,241 --> 00:37:56,340
was our number one priority.

675
00:37:56,341 --> 00:37:58,840
We had gone through the exhaustive process

676
00:37:58,841 --> 00:38:01,740
that NASA goes through to identify the hazards

677
00:38:01,741 --> 00:38:03,840
and to deal with them one by one.

678
00:38:03,841 --> 00:38:06,940
We also asked Mike Fole, is this a safe thing?

679
00:38:07,040 --> 00:38:11,340
Should we be bringing Dave Wolf up or not?

680
00:38:11,440 --> 00:38:15,180
And we talked about specific criteria that needed to be met.

681
00:38:15,280 --> 00:38:16,680
They were met.

682
00:38:16,780 --> 00:38:19,480
Dave went up and had a very successful and productive time,

683
00:38:19,580 --> 00:38:20,960
and Andy followed him.

684
00:38:21,060 --> 00:38:24,300
In 1997, astronaut Andy Thomas

685
00:38:24,400 --> 00:38:28,560
returned to Earth after more than 130 days in space,

686
00:38:28,660 --> 00:38:31,360
ending the phase one program.

687
00:38:31,361 --> 00:38:35,740
In total, seven astronauts stayed 907 days in space

688
00:38:35,840 --> 00:38:39,140
conducting experiments.

689
00:38:39,240 --> 00:38:42,580
We did put some crew members at risk,

690
00:38:42,680 --> 00:38:44,700
and that's part of space flight.

691
00:38:44,800 --> 00:38:47,680
We could have said it isn't safe enough and quit.

692
00:38:47,780 --> 00:38:48,580
We didn't do that.

693
00:38:48,680 --> 00:38:51,080
We showed confidence in our partner,

694
00:38:51,180 --> 00:38:53,180
and we learned a lot out of that.

695
00:38:53,280 --> 00:38:56,220
I think the Shuttle Mirror, the phase one program,

696
00:38:56,320 --> 00:39:00,360
was on balance a very positive success

697
00:39:00,361 --> 00:39:03,060
for the International Space Program

698
00:39:03,061 --> 00:39:07,100
and for the International Space Station.

699
00:39:07,101 --> 00:39:09,300
Russian cosmonauts are paid bonuses

700
00:39:09,301 --> 00:39:11,340
for each activity they successfully

701
00:39:11,341 --> 00:39:13,180
accomplish while in space.

702
00:39:13,181 --> 00:39:15,780
They are also dock pay for each problem.

703
00:39:15,781 --> 00:39:18,380
Space stations will return on Modern Marvels.

704
00:39:23,880 --> 00:39:29,280
We now return to Space Stations on Modern Marvels.

705
00:39:29,281 --> 00:39:34,620
Seven US astronauts lived aboard Mir during the Phase I program.

706
00:39:34,621 --> 00:39:38,400
Despite the system glitches, the fire, and the collision,

707
00:39:38,401 --> 00:39:41,520
the program was dubbed a success.

708
00:39:41,521 --> 00:39:44,980
The US obtained invaluable experience,

709
00:39:44,981 --> 00:39:47,620
not just living in space, but learning

710
00:39:47,621 --> 00:39:52,080
how to deal with unanticipated problems that will inevitably

711
00:39:52,081 --> 00:39:56,660
arise on the International Space Station.

712
00:39:56,661 --> 00:39:59,260
At the end of phase one, the Mir station

713
00:39:59,261 --> 00:40:03,560
had outlived its life expectancy by six years.

714
00:40:03,561 --> 00:40:06,400
NASA urged Russia to terminate Mir

715
00:40:06,401 --> 00:40:09,080
and concentrate their shrinking resources

716
00:40:09,081 --> 00:40:11,400
on the International Space Station.

717
00:40:11,401 --> 00:40:13,480
But Russia was reluctant to give up

718
00:40:13,481 --> 00:40:17,840
the last symbol of its once glorious space program.

719
00:40:17,841 --> 00:40:19,420
One of the outstanding characteristics

720
00:40:19,421 --> 00:40:22,520
of the Russian people is that they don't give up.

721
00:40:22,521 --> 00:40:25,360
They have tremendous personal courage.

722
00:40:25,361 --> 00:40:29,340
By the late 1990s, dwindling budgets seriously threatened

723
00:40:29,440 --> 00:40:30,360
Mir.

724
00:40:30,460 --> 00:40:33,240
For years, the station hosted foreign guests

725
00:40:33,340 --> 00:40:35,900
to raise money for operations.

726
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:40,340
A Japanese journalist paid $12 million to stay nine days

727
00:40:40,440 --> 00:40:41,640
on Mir.

728
00:40:41,740 --> 00:40:44,540
Cosmonauts even resorted to doing commercials

729
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:45,480
while on the station.

730
00:40:55,360 --> 00:40:59,400
In 1999, Russia finally announced plans

731
00:40:59,500 --> 00:41:02,840
to decommission the 13-year-old station.

732
00:41:02,940 --> 00:41:06,600
The Mir will meet the same fiery death as Skylab did

733
00:41:06,700 --> 00:41:09,980
when it re-enters the Earth's atmosphere.

734
00:41:10,080 --> 00:41:14,040
It was a successful program, but Russia's future in space

735
00:41:14,140 --> 00:41:17,880
would be the International Space Station.

736
00:41:17,980 --> 00:41:21,960
Russia began building the first module for the ISS

737
00:41:21,961 --> 00:41:28,440
building the first module for the ISS in December of 1994.

738
00:41:28,540 --> 00:41:32,600
While that module was launched in November 1998,

739
00:41:32,700 --> 00:41:36,140
and the US module launched two weeks later,

740
00:41:36,240 --> 00:41:41,280
46 separate launches are required to ferry modules and supplies

741
00:41:41,380 --> 00:41:43,780
to the growing station.

742
00:41:43,880 --> 00:41:47,520
The Russian KERS automatic docking system

743
00:41:47,620 --> 00:41:49,960
and the shuttle robotic arm are used

744
00:41:49,961 --> 00:41:52,900
to attach additional modules.

745
00:41:53,000 --> 00:41:57,540
But the station requires a good deal of hands-on construction,

746
00:41:57,640 --> 00:42:02,840
connecting cables, tightening bolts, and fastening lines.

747
00:42:02,940 --> 00:42:07,080
A record 75 spacewalks are scheduled,

748
00:42:07,180 --> 00:42:11,140
more than all previous US and Russian missions combined.

749
00:42:14,580 --> 00:42:16,880
The station's appearance resembles

750
00:42:16,980 --> 00:42:19,960
the modular design of the Mir, with seven

751
00:42:19,961 --> 00:42:23,660
In our design, we put the solar rays out at the end of the truss.

752
00:42:23,760 --> 00:42:26,760
The solar rays will basically track the sun.

753
00:42:26,860 --> 00:42:28,700
The station will fly around the Earth

754
00:42:28,800 --> 00:42:30,800
such that the bottom of the station always

755
00:42:30,900 --> 00:42:32,280
points at the Earth.

756
00:42:32,380 --> 00:42:37,620
And the solar rays will rotate on large alpha bearings

757
00:42:37,720 --> 00:42:40,520
that will allow them to track the sun continuously

758
00:42:40,620 --> 00:42:41,720
and generate more power.

759
00:42:41,820 --> 00:42:44,360
The inside of the space station, from a crew's point of view,

760
00:42:44,460 --> 00:42:48,660
is we have a lot more volume, bright, airy spaces,

761
00:42:48,661 --> 00:42:53,200
good food preparation, sleeping facilities.

762
00:42:53,300 --> 00:42:57,060
The human factors are very good overall.

763
00:42:57,160 --> 00:43:01,300
Eventually, the ISS will grow to four times the size

764
00:43:01,400 --> 00:43:04,400
of Mir and offer a lot more room.

765
00:43:04,500 --> 00:43:08,320
But more room won't be enough in the isolation of space.

766
00:43:08,420 --> 00:43:13,220
Training is essential, and patience a necessity.

767
00:43:13,320 --> 00:43:15,180
When you first get on board a space station,

768
00:43:15,280 --> 00:43:17,260
you're faced with a daunting prospect

769
00:43:17,261 --> 00:43:21,001
daunting prospect of spending four or five months there as I was.

770
00:43:21,002 --> 00:43:22,900
You have to make a big adjustment

771
00:43:22,901 --> 00:43:27,140
into your lifestyle to accept the environment that you're in, to accept the confinement.

772
00:43:28,320 --> 00:43:29,801
And that's very hard to do.

773
00:43:29,802 --> 00:43:33,420
You have to set up a place where you're living and you set up some

774
00:43:33,421 --> 00:43:37,595
personal effects and create your small habitation area for yourself.

775
00:43:37,596 --> 00:43:39,560
And you learn to, after a while,

776
00:43:39,880 --> 00:43:46,280
get your personal needs met by the limited resources around you, needs of recreation and so on, as well

777
00:43:46,281 --> 00:43:49,920
as intellectual stimulation.

778
00:43:49,921 --> 00:43:52,520
Once you're able to do that, the time you spend up there

779
00:43:52,521 --> 00:43:55,880
becomes very rewarding and very fulfilling.

780
00:43:55,881 --> 00:43:57,800
The International Space Station

781
00:43:57,801 --> 00:44:02,720
promises advances in the fields of medicine, chemistry,

782
00:44:02,721 --> 00:44:06,200
agriculture, and astronomy.

783
00:44:06,201 --> 00:44:09,080
Our space program has been one of the chief incubators

784
00:44:09,081 --> 00:44:11,440
of new technology in this country.

785
00:44:11,441 --> 00:44:15,020
It's been estimated there are more than 80,000 products

786
00:44:15,021 --> 00:44:18,560
in our everyday way of life that owes its life in some way

787
00:44:18,561 --> 00:44:20,620
right back to the space program.

788
00:44:20,621 --> 00:44:24,260
The ISS may even help humankind understand

789
00:44:24,261 --> 00:44:26,540
more about itself.

790
00:44:26,541 --> 00:44:28,340
I would not for one moment tell you

791
00:44:28,341 --> 00:44:31,160
that we won't have substantial surprises

792
00:44:31,161 --> 00:44:32,660
before we're finished.

793
00:44:32,661 --> 00:44:35,380
And that's just part of the job.

794
00:44:35,381 --> 00:44:38,960
We're doing something no one has ever done before.

795
00:44:38,961 --> 00:44:41,420
Somewhere in our future, we're going to go back to the moon,

796
00:44:41,421 --> 00:44:42,440
go to Mars.

797
00:44:42,441 --> 00:44:44,880
Let's start laying the technological foundation

798
00:44:44,980 --> 00:44:46,480
for that right now, because there's

799
00:44:46,580 --> 00:44:49,020
going to be two things again that are going to happen.

800
00:44:49,120 --> 00:44:51,520
You're going to have output of new knowledge,

801
00:44:51,620 --> 00:44:55,120
and then you're going to have spin off of new technology.

802
00:44:55,220 --> 00:44:58,080
And any time you push that hard for a new goal,

803
00:44:58,180 --> 00:45:01,920
you're going to have some assets that come out of that.

804
00:45:02,020 --> 00:45:05,860
In 1869, Edward Everett Hale predicted

805
00:45:05,960 --> 00:45:10,600
the first space station would be used as a navigational aid.

806
00:45:10,601 --> 00:45:16,540
Finally, the ISS may be Hale's brick moon.

807
00:45:16,640 --> 00:45:19,180
Because of its size, it also has a tremendous amount

808
00:45:19,280 --> 00:45:21,080
of reflective surface.

809
00:45:21,180 --> 00:45:23,880
And so when it goes over in the evening after sunset

810
00:45:23,980 --> 00:45:25,940
or in the morning before sunrise,

811
00:45:26,040 --> 00:45:28,520
there is going to be this huge beacon in the sky

812
00:45:28,620 --> 00:45:31,960
that we're going to walk out and see go over our heads

813
00:45:32,060 --> 00:45:35,700
that we as Earthlings, not as Americans and not as Russians

814
00:45:35,800 --> 00:45:38,160
and not as Japanese, but as Earthlings,

815
00:45:38,260 --> 00:45:40,600
we can look up and take pride in and say, that's ours.

